<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex303.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{c_{3}}\biggl(\biggl|g\bigl(X_{c}^{(2)},\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)},\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}\bigr)-g\bigl(X_{c}^{(2)},{p}_{c}^{(2)},{q}_{c}^{(2)}\bigr)\biggr|+\biggl|g\bigl(X_{c}^{(1)},\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)},\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}\bigr)-g\bigl(X_{c}^{(1)},{p}_{c}^{(1)},{q}_{c}^{(1)}\bigr)\biggr|\biggr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">(</mo><msubsup><mi>X</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo maxsize="1.200em" minsize="1.200em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{c_{3}}\biggl(\biggl|g\bigl(X_{c}^{(2)},\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)},\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}\bigr)-g\bigl(X_{c}^{(2)},{p}_{c}^{(2)},{q}_{c}^{(2)}\bigr)\biggr|+\biggl|g\bigl(X_{c}^{(1)},\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)},\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}\bigr)-g\bigl(X_{c}^{(1)},{p}_{c}^{(1)},{q}_{c}^{(1)}\bigr)\biggr|\biggr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex304"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex304.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{c_{3}^{3}}\biggl(|\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)}-{p}_{c}^{(1)}|+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}-{q}_{c}^{(1)}|+|\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)}-{p}_{c}^{(2)}|+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}-{q}_{c}^{(2)}|\biggr)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{1}{c_{3}^{3}}\biggl(|\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)}-{p}_{c}^{(1)}|+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}-{q}_{c}^{(1)}|+|\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)}-{p}_{c}^{(2)}|+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}-{q}_{c}^{(2)}|\biggr)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.13" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p1.13.1" class="ltx_text">where the first inequality is by triangular inequality, the second is by Cauchy-Schwartz inequality, the third is by Equation (<a href="#A2.E11" title="In Proof. ‣ S2.7 Proof of Theorem (Asymptotic Normality) ‣ Appendix S2 Theoretical Results ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S11</span></a>), and the last is by Equation (<a href="#A2.E10" title="In Proof. ‣ S2.7 Proof of Theorem (Asymptotic Normality) ‣ Appendix S2 Theoretical Results ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S10</span></a>). Now, by Cauchy-Schwartz,</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx125" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex305"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex305.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle(a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}+a_{4})^{2}=\biggl(\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}&amp;a_{2}&amp;a_{3}&amp;a_{4}\end{bmatrix}^{\scriptscriptstyle\mathsf{T}}}_{:=\bm{a}}\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;1&amp;1\end{bmatrix}}_{:=\mathbf{1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\mathsf{T}}}\biggr)^{2}\leq\lVert\bm{a}\rVert^{2}\lVert\mathbf{1}\rVert^{2}=4(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+a_{3}^{2}+a_{4}^{2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>=</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><munder><munder accentunder="true"><msup><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mi mathsize="0.710em">𝖳</mi></msup><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><mi>𝒂</mi></mrow></mrow></munder><munder><munder accentunder="true"><mrow><mo>[</mo><mtable columnspacing="5pt"><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>]</mo></mrow><mo stretchy="true">⏟</mo></munder><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo lspace="0.278em" rspace="0.278em">:</mo><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>=</mo><msup><mn>𝟏</mn><mi>𝖳</mi></msup></mrow></mrow></munder><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo rspace="0.1389em">≤</mo><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0.1389em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mi>𝒂</mi><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mrow><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0em">∥</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><mo fence="true" lspace="0em" rspace="0.1389em">∥</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo lspace="0.1389em">=</mo><mn>4</mn><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo>+</mo><msubsup><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle(a_{1}+a_{2}+a_{3}+a_{4})^{2}=\biggl(\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}a_{1}&amp;a_{2}&amp;a_{3}&amp;a_{4}\end{bmatrix}^{\scriptscriptstyle\mathsf{T}}}_{:=\bm{a}}\underbrace{\begin{bmatrix}1&amp;1&amp;1&amp;1\end{bmatrix}}_{:=\mathbf{1}^{\scriptscriptstyle\mathsf{T}}}\biggr)^{2}\leq\lVert\bm{a}\rVert^{2}\lVert\mathbf{1}\rVert^{2}=4(a_{1}^{2}+a_{2}^{2}+a_{3}^{2}+a_{4}^{2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.14" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p1.14.1" class="ltx_text">for any <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m16" class="ltx_Math" alttext="a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},a_{4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>,</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">a_{1},a_{2},a_{3},a_{4}</annotation></semantics></math>. Applying this, we obtain</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx126" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex306"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_right ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex306.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\biggl|\hat{M}_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})-M_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})\biggr|^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\biggl|\hat{M}_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})-M_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})\biggr|^{2}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex306.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{c_{3}^{6}}\biggl(|\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)}-{p}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}-{q}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}+|\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)}-{p}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}-{q}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}\biggr)." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><mrow><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>+</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{c_{3}^{6}}\biggl(|\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)}-{p}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}-{q}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}+|\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)}-{p}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}+|\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}-{q}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}\biggr).</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.15" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p1.15.1" class="ltx_text">By taking the expectation, we finally get</span></p>
<table id="A2.EGx127" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_align ltx_eqn_table">

<tbody id="A2.Ex307"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex307.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\biggl[\biggl|\hat{M}_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})-M_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})\biggr|^{2}\biggr]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>M</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>−</mo><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>,</mo><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em" stretchy="true">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\mathbb{E}\biggl[\biggl|\hat{M}_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})-M_{c}(\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)},\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)})\biggr|^{2}\biggr]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex308"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex308.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{c_{3}^{6}}\biggl\{\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)}-{p}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}\biggr]+\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}-{q}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}\biggr]+\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)}-{p}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}\biggr]+\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}-{q}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}\biggr]\biggr\}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mphantom></mphantom><mo>≤</mo><mrow><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mrow><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>p</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>p</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow><mo>+</mo><mrow><mi>𝔼</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">[</mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo><mrow><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>q</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>−</mo><msubsup><mi>q</mi><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow><mo stretchy="false">|</mo></mrow><mn>2</mn></msup><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><mo stretchy="false">}</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\leq\frac{4}{c_{3}^{6}}\biggl\{\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{p}_{c}^{(1)}-{p}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}\biggr]+\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{q}_{c}^{(1)}-{q}_{c}^{(1)}|^{2}\biggr]+\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{p}_{c}^{(2)}-{p}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}\biggr]+\mathbb{E}\biggl[|\hat{q}_{c}^{(2)}-{q}_{c}^{(2)}|^{2}\biggr]\biggr\}</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
<tbody id="A2.Ex309"><tr class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A2.Ex309.m1" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="\displaystyle=\frac{4}{c_{3}^{6}}\biggl(o_{p}(n^{-1/2})+o_{p}(n^{-1/2})+o_{p}(n^{-1/2})+o_{p}(n^{-1/2})\biggr)=o_{p}(n^{-1/2})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo>=</mo><mstyle displaystyle="true"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mn>3</mn><mn>6</mn></msubsup></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">(</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><mo maxsize="2.100em" minsize="2.100em">)</mo></mrow><mo>=</mo><msub><mi>o</mi><mi>p</mi></msub><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle=\frac{4}{c_{3}^{6}}\biggl(o_{p}(n^{-1/2})+o_{p}(n^{-1/2})+o_{p}(n^{-1/2})+o_{p}(n^{-1/2})\biggr)=o_{p}(n^{-1/2})</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A2.SS7.p1.16" class="ltx_p"><span id="A2.SS7.p1.16.1" class="ltx_text">where the last line is by using Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption10" title="Assumption 10 (Regularity Conditions) ‣ 4.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 4 The Proposed Methodology with Noisy Human Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. Hence, combined with the first inequality we derived, we prove that the outcome model with the estimated surrogate outcomes achieves <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m17" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> convergence under all the stated conditions at the convergence rate <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m18" class="ltx_math_unparsed" alttext="n^{-1/4}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>n</mi><msup><mrow></mrow><mrow><mo>−</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>/</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">n^{-1/4}</annotation></semantics></math>, which in the end implies the rate double robustness we have in Assumption <a href="#Thmassumption5" title="Assumption 5 (Regularity Conditions) ‣ 3.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 3 The Proposed Methodology with Perfect Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>. The exact same proof applies to the part that the <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m19" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L^{2}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msup><mi>L</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L^{2}</annotation></semantics></math> norm is bounded by the constant <math id="A2.SS7.p1.m20" class="ltx_Math" alttext="c_{1}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">c_{1}</annotation></semantics></math>. As the efficient influence function takes mathematically equivalent expressions, the rest of the proof follows directly from Theorem <a href="#Thmtheorem2" title="Theorem 2 (Asymptotic Normality) ‣ 3.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 3 The Proposed Methodology with Perfect Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>.
∎</span></p>
</div>
</div>
<div class="ltx_pagination ltx_role_newpage"></div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3" class="ltx_appendix">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_appendix"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_appendix">Appendix S3 </span>Simulation Studies</h2>

<section id="A3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.1 </span>Case 1: Perfect Human Annotations</h3>

<section id="A3.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">S3.1.1 </span>Simulation setup</h4>

<div id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.2" class="ltx_p">I conduct simulation studies to evaluate the empirical performance of the proposed SRI estimator and compare it with existing estimators. Given that it is extremely difficult to generate texts that are influenced by predictors in a synthesized experiment, I instead generate high-dimensional vectors that have similar dimensions to standard sentence embeddings and make them influenced by the predictors in the data-generating process. Specifically, I generate data with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=20,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=20,000</annotation></semantics></math> units using the following data-generating process:</p>
<table id="A3.E12" class="ltx_equationgroup ltx_eqn_table">
<tbody>
<tr id="A3.E12X" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E12X.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle T_{i}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.5)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Bernoulli</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle T_{i}\sim\mathrm{Bernoulli}(0.5)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td>
<td rowspan="5" class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_eqno ltx_align_middle ltx_align_right"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_equationgroup ltx_align_right">(S12)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E12Xa" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E12Xa.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\text{For }j\in[1,\cdots,2048]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mtext>For </mtext><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow><mo>∈</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>,</mo><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">⋯</mo><mo>,</mo><mn>2048</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\text{For }j\in[1,\cdots,2048]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E12Xb" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E12Xb.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\alpha_{1j}\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0.0,1.0]" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi>Unif</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">[</mo><mrow><mn>0.0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">]</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\alpha_{1j}\sim\mathrm{Unif}[0.0,1.0]</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E12Xc" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E12Xc.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle Y_{ij}=\alpha_{1j}T_{i}+\epsilon_{i},\quad\epsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>Y</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>=</mo><mrow><mrow><msub><mi>α</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></mrow><mo rspace="1.167em">,</mo><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>∼</mo><mrow><mi class="ltx_font_mathcaligraphic">𝒩</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle Y_{ij}=\alpha_{1j}T_{i}+\epsilon_{i},\quad\epsilon_{i}\sim\mathcal{N}(0,1)</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr>
<tr id="A3.E12Xd" class="ltx_equation ltx_eqn_row ltx_align_baseline">
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padleft"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_eqn_cell"></td>
<td class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_eqn_cell"><math id="A3.E12Xd.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\displaystyle\tilde{L}_{i}=\mathbbm{1}\{\sigma(1.0+0.2\cdot\mathbf{1}^{\intercal}\bm{Y}_{i})&gt;0.5\}." display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>𝟙</mn><mo stretchy="false">{</mo><mi>σ</mi><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1.0</mn><mo>+</mo><mn>0.2</mn><mo lspace="0.222em" rspace="0.222em">⋅</mo><mn>𝟏</mn><msup><mrow></mrow><mo>⊺</mo></msup><mi>𝒀</mi><msub><mrow></mrow><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>0.5</mn><mo stretchy="false">}</mo><mo lspace="0em">.</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\displaystyle\tilde{L}_{i}=\mathbbm{1}\{\sigma(1.0+0.2\cdot\mathbf{1}^{\intercal}\bm{Y}_{i})&gt;0.5\}.</annotation></semantics></math></td>
<td class="ltx_eqn_cell ltx_eqn_center_padright"></td></tr></tbody>
</table>
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.3" class="ltx_p">I then artificially simulate machine-predicted labels <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with accuracies ranging from 70% to 90%, randomly changing the value of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> according to the selected error percentage (i.e., 10% of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> values are randomly changed for <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> if the accuracy of <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is set to 90%).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To implement the proposed SRI estimator, I use 2-fold cross-fitting with the neural network architecture illustrated in Figure <a href="#S3.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 3.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 3 The Proposed Methodology with Perfect Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>. Specifically, the neural network architecture has two layers with dimensions 100 and 50 leading to the shared layer <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}(\bm{R}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}(\bm{R}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The shared layer is then connected to prediction heads for both the outcome and the predictor of interest, each consisting of two layers with dimensions 50 and 1 (i.e., predicting a scalar at last). I use ReLU activations and solve the optimization problem in Equation <a href="#S3.E5" title="In 3.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 3 The Proposed Methodology with Perfect Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> with <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\alpha=1" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>α</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\alpha=1</annotation></semantics></math> using the Adam optimizer with a learning rate of 0.00002, a maximum of 200 epochs, and a batch size of 256 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kingma and Ba,, 2017</a>)</cite>. I use 20% of the input as validation data and implement early stopping with a patience of 5 epochs to prevent overfitting (i.e., if the loss computed on the validation data does not decrease for five consecutive epochs, we stop training). Once the outcome model and surrogacy score are estimated, their estimates are used to calculate the difference in outcome means using the estimating equation in Equation (<a href="#S3.E7" title="In item 3 ‣ 3.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 3 The Proposed Methodology with Perfect Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>).</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As in the empirical application in Section <a href="#S5" title="5 Empirical Application ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>, I evaluate the performance of the proposed estimator by comparing it to the existing bias correction techniques <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egami et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egami et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. Note that I omit any control variables <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> from this simulation for ease of comparison. Because the proposed estimator must estimate the propensity score when <math id="A3.SS1.SSS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{Z}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>𝒁</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{Z}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> is included, differences in efficiency may arise from this additional estimation step, whereas the alternative methods do not require it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib32" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hirano et al.,, 2003</a>)</cite>. Given the simple data‑generating process in this simulation, I can clearly assess how much the independence constraint improves efficiency.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">S3.1.2 </span>Simulation results</h4>

<figure id="A3.F1" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12416v1/sim_perfect.svg" id="A3.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:626/302;" width="626" height="302"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S1</span>: </span><span id="A3.F1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison between the Surrogate Representation Inference (SRI, proposed) and the existing bias-correction algorithm that corrects measurement error from machine learning predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egami et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egami et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. We use the human annotations with no mistakes, and vary the accuracy of machine learning predictions (from 0.70 to 0.90). All results are based on 200 Monte Carlo simulations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F1" title="Figure S1 ‣ S3.1.2 Simulation results ‣ S3.1 Case 1: Perfect Human Annotations ‣ Appendix S3 Simulation Studies ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S1</span></a> presents the results of the simulation studies with perfect human-annotated labels based on 200 Monte Carlo trials. These results demonstrate that the proposed SRI method outperforms the existing bias-correction techniques across all accuracy levels of the proxies <math id="A3.SS1.SSS2.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. As theoretically predicted, the naïve estimator exhibits substantial absolute bias that increases as proxy accuracy decreases. While the existing approaches correct for prediction errors, it suffers from large root mean squared errors (RMSE), particularly when accuracy ranges from 70% to 80%. Consequently, the resulting standard errors are also inflated. For instance, at 80% accuracy, the average standard error for the existing approach is 0.019, compared to 0.012 for the proposed SRI estimator. This indicates that even at moderately high accuracy levels, such as 80%, our estimator significantly reduces the standard error. Therefore, the results confirm that the SRI estimator offers the useful alternative to the existing bias-correction techniques, especially when researchers only have the machine learning predictions with moderate accuracy.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">S3.2 </span>Case 2: Human Annotations with Non-Differential Errors</h3>

<section id="A3.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">S3.2.1 </span>Simulation setup</h4>

<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Next, I explore the validity of the proposed methodology in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 The Proposed Methodology with Noisy Human Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> under the setting where researchers do not have access to the observed label <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> but only have access to two proxies <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>. To do this, I use the same data generating process in Equation (<a href="#A3.E12" title="In S3.1.1 Simulation setup ‣ S3.1 Case 1: Perfect Human Annotations ‣ Appendix S3 Simulation Studies ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S12</span></a>) with <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=20,000" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>20,000</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=20,000</annotation></semantics></math> but without using the true human-annotated label <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>. Specifically, I artificially generate two imperfect human-annotated labels, <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}_{i}^{(1)}</annotation></semantics></math> and <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m7" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msubsup><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>~</mo></mover><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></msubsup><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\tilde{L}_{i}^{(2)}</annotation></semantics></math>, by randomly changing the value of <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m8" class="ltx_Math" alttext="L_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mi>L</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">L_{i}</annotation></semantics></math> with accuracy ranging from 85% to 95% (in the same way as when creating the machine learning predictions <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p1.m9" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\hat{L}_{i}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><msub><mover accent="true"><mi>L</mi><mo>^</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\hat{L}_{i}</annotation></semantics></math>). Since I control the accuracy of the imperfect labels and the errors are generated randomly, all the assumptions introduced in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 The Proposed Methodology with Noisy Human Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> are satisfied.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For estimation, I implement the proposed methodology with a 5-fold cross-fitting procedure introduced in Section <a href="#S4" title="4 The Proposed Methodology with Noisy Human Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a>. Specifically, I first obtain the coder classification probability using half of the sample in each fold and train the neural network using the other half. As in the previous case, the neural network I use has two layers with the same dimensions leading to the shared layer <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\bm{f}(\bm{R}_{i})" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>𝒇</mi><mo>⁡</mo><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><msub><mi>𝑹</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\bm{f}(\bm{R}_{i})</annotation></semantics></math>. The shared layer is then connected to the prediction head for the predictor of interest, the human-annotated label, and the outcome for each category class. For each head, I use the same architecture as in the previous case, consisting of two layers with dimensions 50 and 1. I use ReLU activations and solve the optimization problem in Equation (<a href="#S4.E16" title="In 4.2 Estimation and inference ‣ 4 The Proposed Methodology with Noisy Human Annotations ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>) with <math id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p2.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\beta=\gamma=1.0" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>β</mi><mo>=</mo><mi>γ</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1.0</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\beta=\gamma=1.0</annotation></semantics></math>. I use the Adam optimizer with the same hyperparameters and early stopping options.</p>
</div>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">As with the previous case, I compare the proposed SRI approach with the existing bias-correction approach, but this time I use noisy human-annotated labels combined with machine learning predictions. Since two noisy human-annotated labels are artificially generated, I use the first one as the human-annotated labels for the existing approaches. For machine learning predictions, as in the previous case, I vary the accuracy of the machine learning predictions from 70% to 90%. Since researchers do not have access to the ground-truth human-annotated labels, it is expected that all estimators except the proposed SRI estimator would suffer from substantial bias and large RMSE.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="A3.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">S3.2.2 </span>Simulation results</h4>

<figure id="A3.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.12416v1/sim_imperfect.svg" id="A3.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:626/477;" width="626" height="477"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="A3.F2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure S2</span>: </span><span id="A3.F2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Performance comparison between the Surrogate Representation Inference (SRI, proposed) and the existing bias-correction algorithm that corrects measurement error from machine learning predictions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bib2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angelopoulos et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egami et al.,, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bib16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Egami et al.,, 2024</a>)</cite>. We use the human annotations with non-differential errors, and vary both the accuracy of machine learning predictions (from 0.70 to 0.90, x-axis) and the accuracy of human annotations (from 0.85 to 0.95, y-axis). All results are based on 200 Monte Carlo simulations.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="A3.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#A3.F2" title="Figure S2 ‣ S3.2.2 Simulation results ‣ S3.2 Case 2: Human Annotations with Non-Differential Errors ‣ Appendix S3 Simulation Studies ‣ Surrogate Representation Inference for Noisy Text and Image Annotations" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">S2</span></a> presents the results of the simulation study, which is based on 200 Monte Carlo trials and uses two human‑annotated labels that contain non‑differential errors. These results show that the proposed SRI methodology successfully corrects the bias introduced by the human annotations, yielding smaller absolute bias and RMSE across all levels of machine‑learning prediction accuracy. Unlike in the previous scenario, the performance of the existing bias‑correction approaches do not improve, even as the quality of machine‑learning predictions increases, because the human annotations contain unknown non‑differential errors. On the other hand, across all settings, the SRI method generally produces larger standard errors than the existing bias‑correction approaches, especially when the accuracy of the human annotations is lower. This occurs because the proposed estimator infers the true labels from multiple noisy annotations; when those annotations contain more errors, the resulting estimates become noisier. Therefore, although the proposed SRI estimator is theoretically guaranteed to remain unbiased in the presence of random annotation errors, researchers should still make an effort to obtain high‑quality human annotations to minimize the standard error.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
